// Wonder Lab human-body data. Generated by tools/build_extras.py
// ids are stable and referenced by the Try It Now tracker — never
// renumber an existing one.
// 164 facts across 14 sections.
const BODY = [{"id":"b45","section":"defence","cat":"weird","text":"A fever is not the illness. It is your body deliberately turning the heat up.","more":"Many germs breed best at your normal temperature, and your immune cells work faster when it is warmer. Your body raises the thermostat on purpose.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b46","section":"defence","cat":"defence","text":"White blood cells hunt. Some chase bacteria down and swallow them whole.","more":"The cell flows around the invader, engulfs it, and breaks it apart inside. This is called phagocytosis — literally \"cell eating\".","kind":"found"},{"id":"b47","section":"defence","cat":"gross","text":"A scab is a temporary lid built out of your own blood.","more":"Tiny cells called platelets clump at the tear. They lay down a mesh of fibers, and blood cells get caught in it. The whole plug dries hard. New skin is built underneath.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b48","section":"defence","cat":"weird","text":"Sneezes leave your nose at genuinely impressive speed, and you cannot keep your eyes open for one.","more":"The eye-closing is a reflex bundled with the sneeze. Nobody has found a good reason for it — your eyes would not pop out, despite the playground legend.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b49","section":"defence","cat":"oops","text":"Your immune system is built to fight off germs. An allergy is that same system attacking something harmless by mistake.","more":"Pollen and peanuts are not dangerous. The itching, the swelling and the sneezing are entirely your own defenses overreacting.","kind":"found"},{"section":"defence","cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"Green snot does not mean you need antibiotics. The color comes from an enzyme carried inside your own white blood cells, and it shows up whether the trouble is a virus or a bacterium.","id":"b105"},{"section":"defence","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Milkmaids who had caught cowpox seemed never to catch smallpox. In 1796 Edward Jenner tested it: he scratched cowpox fluid into the arm of an eight-year-old, James Phipps, and later exposed him to smallpox. Phipps did not get ill.","more":"Smallpox has since been wiped off the planet completely, the only human disease that has. The word vaccine comes from vacca, the Latin for cow.","id":"b106"},{"section":"defence","cat":"defence","kind":"record","text":"Alexander Fleming had a cold, and a drop from his nose landed on a dish of bacteria. Where the drop sat, the bacteria dissolved. He had stumbled on a germ-killer that your tears, spit and snot produce all the time.","more":"He named it lysozyme in 1922, six years before the mold that gave him penicillin. To get more tears for testing, he had volunteers squeeze lemon peel in front of their eyes.","id":"b107"},{"section":"defence","cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"Pus is mostly white blood cells that died doing their job, mixed with the germs they killed and the bits of tissue wrecked in the fight.","id":"b108"},{"section":"defence","cat":"created","kind":"found","text":"Air and food both come in through the same doorway, and a ring of immune tissue is stationed exactly there. Your tonsils are part of it — guard posts placed at the one opening everything has to pass through.","more":"Open wide in front of a mirror and the two lumps at the back on either side are the tonsils. They are constantly sampling what you breathe and swallow, which is also why they are often the first thing to swell.","id":"b109"},{"section":"defence","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Being sick is a defense, not a malfunction. A small patch at the base of your brain samples the blood for poisons, and if it finds one it empties your stomach without consulting you.","id":"b110"},{"section":"defence","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"You can feel your own immune system's inspection points.","tryit":"Press gently under your jaw, just behind the corner, and roll your fingers around. Small firm beads move under the skin: lymph nodes. Fluid drained out of your head passes through them to be checked, and they swell when you are fighting something off.","id":"b111"},{"section":"cells","cat":"size","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody has ever counted the cells in a body. The best estimate measures the size and packing of about 1,200 kinds of cell across 60 tissues, then adds it all up. That gives roughly 36 trillion in an adult man. It gives 28 trillion in an adult woman, and about 17 trillion in a ten-year-old.","more":"Nine out of ten of those are red blood cells, which are tiny. Count by weight instead of by number and muscle and fat take over completely. Which answer is the right one depends entirely on what you are asking.","id":"b128"},{"section":"cells","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Coiled inside almost every one of your cells is about 6 ft (2 m) of DNA, spelled out in an alphabet of four letters and three billion letters long. All of it is folded into a nucleus far too small to see.","id":"b129"},{"section":"cells","cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Every cell carries the whole instruction book and reads only its own chapter. A skin cell holds the full directions for building a heart and never opens them. A code that stores itself, copies itself and checks its own spelling reads like something written, not something spilled.","id":"b130"},{"section":"cells","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Your longest cell runs from the base of your spine all the way down to your big toe. That is over 3 ft (1 m) of one single cell.","more":"At the other end of the scale, you could line about ten red blood cells side by side across the width of one hair from your head. Both are cells, and one is a million times bigger than the other.","id":"b131"},{"section":"cells","cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"You throw away and rebuild around 330 billion cells a day. The lining of your gut is the quickest of the lot, swapping its whole surface out every four or five days. Red blood cells last about four months.","id":"b132"},{"section":"cells","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"You are about 60% water. Your brain and your heart are 73% water, your lungs 83%, your skin 64%, and even your bones come in at 31%.","more":"Babies are the wettest of all, roughly 78% water at birth and drying out to about 65% by their first birthday. That is part of why a small child gets dangerously thirsty far faster than a grown-up does.","id":"b133"},{"section":"cells","cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A few of your cells are the originals. The ones in the middle of the lens of each eye formed before you were born. They are never swapped out. They have to stay perfectly see-through for a whole lifetime.","id":"b134"},{"section":"cells","cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Inside each cell sit hundreds of tiny power plants called mitochondria. They turn food and oxygen into energy the cell can use. A heart muscle cell crams in thousands of them, filling about a third of the cell. It never gets a night off.","id":"b135"},{"section":"cells","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Hold your breath and you soon feel a desperate need to gasp. That is mostly your body reacting to waste gas piling up. It is not you running out of oxygen.","tryit":"Breathe in, hold it, and count. Then breathe all the way out and hold that, and count again. The empty one is far shorter. With no air in your lungs to dilute it, the carbon dioxide your cells keep making builds up much faster.","id":"b136"},{"section":"hands","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"There are no muscles inside your fingers. Every bend and straighten is worked from a distance, by muscles in your palm and forearm hauling on cords called tendons that run down inside each finger.","tryit":"Wrap your left hand around your right forearm just below the elbow, then wiggle your right fingers. You can feel the muscles doing the job several inches away from the fingers that are actually moving.","id":"b146"},{"section":"hands","cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Your fingerprints were finished before you were born. The ridges buckle into place between about the tenth and sixteenth week in the womb. After that they never change. Identical twins share the same DNA, and they still have different prints.","tryit":"Scribble a thick patch of pencil on paper, rub a fingertip in it, press clear tape onto the finger and stick the tape down on white paper. You have just lifted your own print.","more":"A handful of families are born with no fingerprints at all, from a single change in one gene. It causes them no health trouble whatsoever. It just makes crossing borders extremely slow.","id":"b147"},{"section":"hands","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"A finger whose nerve has been cut will not wrinkle in water. Doctors use that as a test: warm water for half an hour, and any fingertip that stays smooth has a nerve problem.","tryit":"Fill a bowl with comfortably warm water, around 104°F (40°C), and rest the fingertips of one hand in it. Ridges usually show up somewhere between 10 and 40 minutes. Keep the other hand out and compare.","more":"Photograph a wrinkled finger, dry it off, and soak it again a day later. The loops and ridges land in the same places. The blood vessels underneath barely shift position, so the creases they pull have nowhere else to go.","id":"b148"},{"section":"hands","cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Your little finger does far more than it looks capable of. Measured on a grip meter, leaving it out of your grip costs about a third of your strength. Leaving out the ring finger costs about a fifth.","id":"b149"},{"section":"hands","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"One of your fingers cannot move on its own, and it is not the one you would guess.","tryit":"Put your palm flat on a table and fold your middle finger under, knuckle down on the wood. Now lift each finger in turn. Thumb, index and little finger all come up easily. The ring finger will not budge, because its tendon is tied to the middle finger's.","id":"b150"},{"section":"hands","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Fingernails grow about 0.14 in (3.5 mm) a month, roughly twice as fast as toenails, which manage about 0.06 in (1.6 mm). Your little fingernail is the slowest of the ten.","id":"b151"},{"section":"hands","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Your brain keeps a map of your body. It is wildly out of scale. The hands take up more room on it than your whole trunk and both legs together. The map is drawn by how much control a part needs, not by how big the part is.","more":"A surgeon named Wilder Penfield mapped it during operations. His patients were awake. He touched the surface of the brain with a fine wire and asked what they felt, or watched which part of them twitched.","id":"b152"},{"section":"hands","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Every way you hold anything is one of two grips. A surgeon sorted them out in 1956. In a power grip the fingers wrap round and the palm joins in, the way they do on a hammer. In a precision grip the thing is pinched between thumb and fingertips, the way it is on a pencil.","id":"b153"},{"section":"hands","cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Your thumb can reach around and touch the tip of every other finger on the same hand. That one trick is the difference between a paddle and a tool-holder: thread a needle, hold a pen, do up a button. It is hard to read a hand as anything but purpose-built.","id":"b154"},{"section":"voice","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Your voice is two small flaps in your throat slapping together. A child's voice runs at about 285 slaps a second, an adult woman's around 250, an adult man's around 145. Lower voice, slower flaps.","more":"The flaps grow as you do. The vibrating edge measures about half an inch (12 to 17 mm) in an adult woman and three quarters of an inch (17 to 23 mm) in an adult man. Longer and heavier means slower, and slower means deeper.","id":"b137"},{"section":"voice","cat":"talk","kind":"found","text":"During puberty a boy's vocal folds grow up to 60% longer and thicker. A girl's grow too, by up to about a third. Bigger, heavier flaps swing more slowly, and the voice drops.","more":"The cracking in between is best read as a control problem. The equipment changes size faster than the brain updates its aim, so a note escapes an octave off before the settings catch up.","id":"b138"},{"section":"voice","cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"Newborns cry with an accent. Recordings made in their first days show French babies' cries mostly rising in pitch. German babies' cries mostly fall. Each one matches the tune of the language it had been hearing from inside.","more":"Thirty French and thirty German newborns, 1,254 cries, every one measured for pitch shape. None of them could babble yet, let alone copy a word. The tune arrived first.","id":"b139"},{"section":"voice","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The best way to stop hiccups is to make your diaphragm pull hard while you swallow at the same moment. A stiff straw built to force exactly that stopped hiccups in nearly 92% of the people who tried it.","more":"An American farmer named Charles Osborne started hiccupping while trying to lift a hog. He did not stop for 68 years. That is an estimated 430 million hiccups, and it is the longest bout on record. Nothing anyone tried worked. It finally quit on its own.","id":"b140"},{"section":"voice","cat":"weird","kind":"worked","text":"Nobody has settled why yawning is catching. Cooling the brain, sharpening attention and keeping a group in step have all been argued for, and none of them has enough evidence to close the case. What is not in doubt is that seeing a yawn makes you likelier to do one.","tryit":"Yawn deliberately in front of somebody who is reading, and count how long until they yawn back. It works better on people who know you well, which is itself part of the puzzle.","id":"b141"},{"section":"voice","cat":"teamwork","kind":"found","text":"Laughing is mostly not about jokes. Researchers recorded real conversations and counted. People were around 30 times likelier to laugh with company than alone. Only about one line in eight that got a laugh was even remotely funny.","more":"The speaker usually laughs more than the audience does, dropping laughs into their own sentences like punctuation. People signing rather than speaking put them in the same places.","id":"b142"},{"section":"voice","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The loudest shout Guinness has measured is 122.4 decibels, set by an Australian town crier, Joseph McGrail-Bateup, on the single word 'now'. It left him hoarse for days. The loudest scream on record, 129 decibels, belongs to Jill Drake.","more":"The mark he beat had stood since 1994, when a Northern Irish schoolteacher, Annalisa Flanagan, reached 121.7 decibels shouting the word 'quiet'. McGrail-Bateup needed seven goes to get past her.","id":"b143"},{"section":"voice","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"A whisper does not use your voice at all. The flaps hold still, and you shape the hiss of moving air instead.","tryit":"Rest two fingers lightly on the front of your throat and say your name out loud. You will feel a clear buzz. Now whisper it. The buzzing stops dead, but the words still come through fine.","id":"b144"},{"section":"voice","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"In Genesis the first job handed to a man is naming the animals, one at a time. Speech turns up very early in that account. A newborn arrives with the whole apparatus already assembled. That is long before there is anything to say with it.","id":"b145"},{"id":"b21","section":"lungs","cat":"size","text":"Unfolded flat, the inside surface of your lungs would cover a good chunk of a tennis court.","more":"All that area is folded into your chest as hundreds of millions of tiny air sacs. Each one is thinner than a sheet of tissue paper, so oxygen slips straight through into the blood.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b22","section":"lungs","cat":"tryit","text":"You cannot hum while holding your nose.","tryit":"Try it. Humming needs air moving out through your nose, so pinching it shut stops the sound instantly. Now try to hum and swallow at the same time — also impossible.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b23","section":"lungs","cat":"weird","text":"Your left lung is smaller than your right, because your heart is sitting in the space.","more":"The left lung has two lobes; the right has three. That notch in the left one has a name — the cardiac notch.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b24","section":"lungs","cat":"oops","text":"A hiccup is your diaphragm twitching, and the \"hic\" is your voice box slamming shut.","more":"The diaphragm is the big sheet of muscle under your lungs. When it spasms you gulp air suddenly, and your vocal cords snap closed about a thirty-fifth of a second later. That snap is the sound.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b25","section":"lungs","cat":"samesame","text":"You breathe in and out through the same tubes. A bird's air flows one way, in a loop.","more":"That one-way system keeps fresh air moving across a bird's lungs even while it breathes out. It is part of how some birds fly at heights where a person would black out.","animal":"bar-tailed-godwit","kind":"found"},{"section":"lungs","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Yawning is not your body grabbing extra oxygen. Robert Provine had volunteers breathe pure oxygen, then air loaded with carbon dioxide. Neither changed how much they yawned.","more":"Breathing sped up on the carbon dioxide, as you would expect, but yawns did not. Why we yawn is still open; the one thing that is settled is that the oxygen story is out.","id":"b77"},{"section":"lungs","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"You sigh roughly every five minutes without ever deciding to. A sigh is a double-sized breath, and its job is popping open air sacs that have collapsed shut.","more":"Around half a billion of those sacs line your lungs, and some stick closed every hour. Without the automatic sigh they would stay shut, and the working surface of your lungs would quietly shrink.","id":"b78"},{"section":"lungs","cat":"created","kind":"found","text":"The tiny air sacs in your lungs are wet inside, and wet surfaces stick shut. Each one is coated in a soapy film that stops that happening, so every sac reopens on every single breath.","more":"Babies born very early sometimes have not made the film yet, and their air sacs collapse between breaths. Mary Ellen Avery worked out why in 1959, and the film can now be given as a medicine.","id":"b79"},{"section":"lungs","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"You are mostly breathing through one nostril right now.","tryit":"Hold a cold window or a mirror under your nose and breathe out. One patch of mist comes out bigger. Roughly three quarters of your air goes through the dominant nostril, and your nose swaps which one that is every couple of hours, all day, without telling you.","id":"b80"},{"section":"lungs","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Your breath leaves at one temperature but can feel hot or cold.","tryit":"Hold your palm a few inches from your mouth. Breathe out slowly with your mouth wide open — warm. Now purse your lips and blow hard at the same spot — cold. The fast jet drags cool room air along with it, and that is most of what lands on your hand.","id":"b81"},{"section":"lungs","cat":"talk","kind":"found","text":"You talk on the way out. Air pushed up from your lungs makes two small flaps in your throat flutter — for a child's voice, somewhere around 300 times a second.","id":"b82"},{"section":"lungs","cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"You swallow around 3 pints (1.5 L) of your own mucus a day and never notice. Tiny hairs lining your airways row it steadily upward at about half an inch (1 cm) a minute, carrying trapped dust with it.","id":"b83"},{"id":"b14","section":"bones","cat":"weird","text":"You were born with about 300 bones. You now have 206. You did not lose them — some fused together.","more":"A baby's skull is several separate plates with gaps between them, which is what lets the head flex on the way out and then keep growing. They knit together over the first couple of years.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b15","section":"bones","cat":"size","text":"Your smallest bone is inside your ear and is about the size of a grain of rice.","more":"It is called the stapes, or stirrup, because that is exactly what it looks like. Three tiny bones in a chain amplify the wobble of your eardrum and pass it inward.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b16","section":"bones","cat":"record","text":"Bone is astonishingly strong for its weight — pound for pound, stronger than steel.","more":"Bone manages that by being two things at once. Hard mineral makes it stiff. Stretchy collagen fibers make it tough. Pure mineral would shatter. Pure collagen would simply bend.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b17","section":"bones","cat":"tryit","text":"Your jaw muscle is one of the strongest muscles you own, for its size.","tryit":"Put your fingers on your temples and bite down gently. Feel that bulge? That is your temporalis muscle — it runs up the side of your skull, which is why chewing makes your head move.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b18","section":"bones","cat":"weird","text":"Over half the bones in your body are in your hands and feet.","more":"Twenty-seven in each hand, twenty-six in each foot. All those small bones are what let your hand wrap around a ball and your foot roll through a step.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b19","section":"bones","cat":"samesame","text":"A giraffe has exactly the same number of neck bones as you do: seven.","more":"Theirs are just enormously long. Almost every mammal has seven, from a mouse to a whale.","animal":"giraffe","kind":"found"},{"id":"b20","section":"bones","cat":"oops","text":"Growing pains are real. They are almost certainly not the bone stretching, though. Bone grows far too slowly for anyone to feel it happen.","more":"They are usually muscle aches in the legs after an active day, and they tend to strike in the evening. Bone growth itself is far too slow to feel.","kind":"worked"},{"section":"bones","cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"Cracking your knuckles does not give you arthritis. Donald Unger cracked the knuckles of his left hand at least twice a day for over 60 years and never cracked his right. X-rays of both hands showed no difference.","more":"He collected an Ig Nobel Prize for it in 2009. The pop itself was filmed live in an MRI scanner in 2015: it is a gas pocket suddenly forming as the joint surfaces pull apart, not a bubble bursting.","id":"b70"},{"section":"bones","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Your thigh bone is the longest bone you own, and it comes to close to a quarter of your height. Take your height, divide by four, and you have roughly its length.","id":"b71"},{"section":"bones","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Bone is living tissue, torn down and rebuilt without stopping. One kind of cell chews out old bone, another lays fresh bone into the gap, and over about ten years the whole skeleton gets replaced.","more":"That is why bone thickens where you load it and thins where you do not. Astronauts, with nothing to push against, lose bone quickly and have to exercise for hours a day to slow it down.","id":"b72"},{"section":"bones","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"One bone in your body touches no other bone. The hyoid, a small horseshoe in your throat, hangs in muscle alone, and your tongue is anchored to it.","tryit":"Rest a finger lightly on the soft part of your throat just above your voice box and swallow. The little arch that jumps up and back down under your fingertip is the hyoid, held by nothing but muscle.","id":"b73"},{"section":"bones","cat":"record","kind":"found","text":"Teeth are not bones. Enamel is the hardest material your body makes, harder than any bone you have — and unlike bone it cannot repair itself, which is the entire reason for brushing.","id":"b74"},{"section":"bones","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Your arm is joined to the rest of your skeleton by exactly one bone. Trace the ridge from the notch at the base of your throat out to the point of your shoulder: that collarbone is the only bony link. Everything else is muscle.","id":"b75"},{"section":"bones","cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Your backbone is not a straight column. It curves in at the neck, out at the chest and in again at the waist. A straight spine would send every footfall jarring up into your skull; the S-shape turns it into a spring.","id":"b76"},{"section":"heat","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"98.6°F (37°C) is not your temperature. That number came from one German doctor's readings in 1851. Measured today, the average sits closer to 97.9°F (36.6°C), and yours shifts by about a degree over the course of a day all by itself.","tryit":"Take your temperature first thing in the morning and again around six in the evening. The evening reading is usually the higher one. Your thermostat runs a schedule, and its lowest point falls in the small hours.","id":"b155"},{"section":"heat","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Sweat does nothing at all while it is sitting on you. The cooling only happens as it turns into vapor, which is why a muggy day is so much worse than a dry one.","tryit":"Wet the back of one hand and leave the other dry, then blow steadily across both. The wet one goes noticeably cold. The water is stealing heat from your skin in order to escape as vapor.","more":"You carry somewhere between two and four million sweat glands. Working hard in the heat, a person can pour out a quart or two (1 to 2 liters) an hour. A horse sweats as well. A dog cannot, and has to pant instead.","id":"b156"},{"section":"heat","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The claim that you lose 40% of your heat through your head is wrong. It traces back to a US Army manual from the 1950s, written after tests where volunteers wore arctic suits with only their heads left bare. Your head is about 7% of your skin, and loses roughly that share.","id":"b157"},{"section":"heat","cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"Shivering is your muscles firing against each other on purpose, making heat instead of movement. Going flat out, it lifts your heat production to around five times its resting level.","id":"b158"},{"section":"heat","cat":"senses","kind":"worked","text":"The strangest part of a fever is the shivering at the start. The best explanation is that your thermostat has been reset upward, so your ordinary temperature now reads as far too cold. You feel frozen while your body is deliberately running hot.","id":"b159"},{"section":"heat","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Every hair on you has a tiny muscle attached that yanks it upright. That is a goosebump. The same muscle fires for cold, for fright and for a piece of music. It doubles as the strut holding a nerve against the cells that grow new hair.","more":"Take that muscle away in a mouse and the nerve pulls back and hair growth slows. Leave the mouse in the cold and the nerve fires harder and hair grows faster. The bump you can see is the least of what the muscle is doing.","id":"b160"},{"section":"heat","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The survivable range for a human core is wider than it looks. Anna Bagenholm was pulled out from under river ice at 56.7°F (13.7°C) with no heartbeat, and lived. Willie Jones survived heatstroke at 115.7°F (46.5°C). Both are records. Both people recovered.","id":"b161"},{"section":"heat","cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A newborn barely shivers. Instead it carries a special brown fat, stuffed with power plants, across its back and shoulders, and burns it purely to make heat. Most of that fat quietly fades as a child grows.","id":"b162"},{"section":"heat","cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"You have sweat glands all over you, and a thermostat that holds you inside about a degree. That lets a person keep working through the middle of a hot day. Most animals have to find shade. Genesis puts people in a garden to work it and keep it. The cooling kit suits the job.","id":"b163"},{"id":"b8","section":"heart","cat":"record","text":"Your heart beats roughly 100,000 times a day and has never once taken a day off.","more":"It manages that because it rests between every single beat. Add all those tiny pauses together and the heart is actually \"off duty\" for a large part of every day.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b9","section":"heart","cat":"tryit","text":"You can find your own pulse in about five seconds.","tryit":"Put two fingers — not your thumb, it has its own pulse — on the thumb side of your wrist. Count the beats for 15 seconds and multiply by four. Now run on the spot for a minute and try again.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b10","section":"heart","cat":"size","text":"Your heart is about the size of your own closed fist. It grows as you do, so it always roughly matches.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b11","section":"heart","cat":"weird","text":"Nobody has laid one person's blood vessels end to end. Count the capillaries in a small piece of tissue, multiply up, and you get tens of thousands of miles.","more":"Almost all of that length is capillaries — vessels so narrow that red blood cells have to squeeze through in single file.","kind":"worked"},{"id":"b12","section":"heart","cat":"samesame","text":"Your heart has four chambers. So does a crocodile's — most reptiles have three.","animal":"saltwater-crocodile","kind":"found"},{"id":"b13","section":"heart","cat":"food","text":"Red blood cells carry oxygen using iron. That is genuinely why blood tastes faintly metallic.","kind":"found"},{"section":"heart","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Your blood is never blue. Blood that has given up its oxygen is dark red, and veins only look blue because of how light gets back out of your skin.","more":"Red light travels deep and gets soaked up by the dark blood below. Blue light bounces off the fibers in the upper skin and comes straight back to your eye. The vein is red; the picture is blue.","id":"b63"},{"section":"heart","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"William Harvey settled the question with arithmetic in 1628. He worked out how much blood one beat pushes, multiplied it by the beats in half an hour, and got far more blood than a whole body holds. So it had to be going round in a loop.","more":"Before that, blood was thought to be made fresh in the liver and used up at the edges. Harvey never saw a capillary — no microscope of his was good enough — but the numbers left him no other answer.","id":"b64"},{"section":"heart","cat":"size","kind":"found","text":"Your heart shifts about 5 quarts (5 L) of blood a minute, which is your entire supply. Over a day that adds up to roughly 2,000 gallons (7,600 L).","id":"b65"},{"section":"heart","cat":"created","kind":"found","text":"The lub-dub of a heartbeat is not the squeeze. It is doors slamming: four valves that open one way only, so blood goes forward and can never wash back.","id":"b66"},{"section":"heart","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"You can watch blood refill part of your body in under two seconds.","tryit":"Press hard on a fingernail for five seconds, then let go. The nail is white, then color floods back — normally in under two seconds. Paramedics use this exact test to check that blood is still reaching someone's hands and feet.","id":"b67"},{"section":"heart","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Your heart does not wait to be told. A patch of cells at the top sets the beat, and even single heart cells taken out and kept in a dish carry on beating on their own.","more":"It is why a donated heart starts up again in a new chest. Nerves from the brain speed the beat up or slow it down, but the rhythm itself belongs to the heart.","id":"b68"},{"section":"heart","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"The pipe leaving your heart is called the aorta. It is about 1 inch (2.5 cm) across, near enough a garden hose. It runs more than a foot (30 cm) before it starts to divide.","id":"b69"},{"id":"b0","section":"brain","cat":"weird","text":"Your brain is about 60% fat, which makes it the fattiest organ you own.","more":"That fat is not spare padding — it is structural. Much of it forms the insulating sheath wrapped around your nerve fibers, and the membranes every brain cell is built from.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b1","section":"brain","cat":"speed","text":"Signals race along your nerves at up to about 270 mph (430 km/h) — faster than a Formula 1 car.","more":"Not all nerves are quick. The fibers carrying sharp pain are among the fastest; the ones carrying dull ache crawl along at walking pace. That is why you snatch your hand back from something hot before it properly hurts.","animal":"cheetah","kind":"found"},{"id":"b2","section":"brain","cat":"size","text":"Your brain is roughly the size of two clenched fists held together, and weighs about as much as a small pineapple.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b3","section":"brain","cat":"tryit","text":"Your brain quietly hides a hole in your vision, and you can catch it doing it.","tryit":"Draw a dot and a cross about 6 inches apart on paper. Close your right eye, stare at the cross with your left, and slowly move the paper toward your face. The dot vanishes — that is your blind spot, where the nerve leaves the eye. Your brain paints over the gap so smoothly you never notice.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b4","section":"brain","cat":"tryit","text":"You can make your own arm float upward without trying.","tryit":"Stand in a doorway and press the backs of both hands hard against the frame for 45 seconds. Step forward and relax. Your arms drift up on their own — the muscles are still firing after your brain stopped asking.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b5","section":"brain","cat":"oops","text":"Brain freeze is not your brain getting cold — your brain never cools that fast. The best read is a blood vessel in the roof of your mouth over-reacting to the chill.","more":"Something very cold makes an artery up there narrow, then widen again fast. Nerves nearby report the change. Your brain misreads where that signal came from. So you feel it in your forehead instead of the roof of your mouth.","kind":"worked"},{"id":"b6","section":"brain","cat":"oops","text":"Pins and needles happen when you squash a nerve, not when your foot \"falls asleep\".","more":"Pressure interrupts the nerve's signalling. When you shift and the pressure lifts, the nerve fires off a burst of confused messages all at once — which your brain reads as fizzing.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b7","section":"brain","cat":"sleep","text":"That falling jolt just as you drift off has a name: a hypnic jerk. Most people get them.","more":"Nobody has fully settled why they happen. One well-supported idea is that as your muscles go slack, the change is briefly misread as falling, and your body responds by catching itself.","kind":"found"},{"section":"brain","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"The idea that you use only 10% of your brain is simply wrong. Scans show activity all over it, and there is no spare, unused portion sitting idle.","more":"Damage to almost any small patch costs something — a word, a memory, a movement, a piece of the visual field. If nine tenths were spare, most head injuries would cost nothing at all.","id":"b56"},{"section":"brain","cat":"weird","kind":"record","text":"Nobody had ever actually counted the cells in a human brain. Suzana Herculano-Houzel dissolved brains into a smooth soup. Then she counted the nuclei floating in it. The answer came out near 86 billion neurons. Textbooks had been repeating 100 billion for decades.","id":"b57"},{"section":"brain","cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"Your brain is about 2% of your weight and burns about 20% of your energy — roughly 20 watts, less than an old-style light bulb.","id":"b58"},{"section":"brain","cat":"created","kind":"found","text":"Your brain sits in clear fluid that is almost exactly as dense as brain tissue itself. Floating in it drops the brain's effective weight from about 3 lb (1.4 kg) to roughly 2 oz (50 g) — the difference between resting on bone and hanging weightless.","id":"b59"},{"section":"brain","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"You have never once seen your own eyes move.","tryit":"Stand close to a mirror. Look at your left eye, then your right, then back again. You never catch the movement — your vision shuts off for the instant your eyes jump, so you are not blurred. Get someone else to watch you do it; they see it perfectly clearly.","id":"b60"},{"section":"brain","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"You can give your own foot an order it will refuse to follow.","tryit":"Sit down, lift your right foot off the floor and circle it clockwise. Keep it going, and now draw a big number 6 in the air with your right hand. The foot flips direction. Both jobs are run from the same side of your brain, and the hand wins.","id":"b61"},{"section":"brain","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"London taxi drivers have to learn 25,000 streets before they are licensed. Scans show the back of their hippocampus — the brain's map room — is measurably larger than other drivers', and it grows while they learn.","more":"Trainees who studied for years and then failed the test showed no such growth. The reading is hard to escape: the parts you work hardest are the parts that get built up.","id":"b62"},{"id":"b26","section":"senses","cat":"weird","text":"The tongue map in old textbooks — sweet at the tip, bitter at the back — is simply wrong.","more":"Every part of your tongue can detect every taste. The diagram came from a mistranslation of a German study and then got copied into books for decades.","kind":"record"},{"id":"b27","section":"senses","cat":"tryit","text":"Most of what you call taste is actually smell.","tryit":"Pinch your nose shut and eat a jelly bean without looking. You will get sweet, and almost nothing else. Let go of your nose mid-chew and the flavor arrives all at once.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b28","section":"senses","cat":"tryit","text":"Your sense of temperature is relative, and you can fool it in under a minute.","tryit":"Fill three bowls: cold, lukewarm, warm. Put one hand in cold and one in warm for 30 seconds, then plunge both into the lukewarm one. The same water feels hot to one hand and cold to the other.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b29","section":"senses","cat":"senses","text":"Nobody has sat a person down with a million colors. Measure the smallest change your eye can just catch, multiply it up, and you land at roughly a million shades.","animal":"mantis-shrimp","kind":"worked"},{"id":"b30","section":"senses","cat":"weird","text":"Close your eyes and you still know where your hands are. That is a real sense, called proprioception. Most people never count it.","tryit":"Close your eyes and touch your nose with one finger. You did not need to see your hand — that is proprioception, reporting from every joint and muscle.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b31","section":"senses","cat":"oops","text":"The best read on motion sickness is that your eyes and your ears disagree, and your brain cannot settle the argument.","more":"Reading in a moving car, your eyes report a still page while the balance organs in your inner ear report bouncing. Your brain gets two contradictory stories and responds by making you feel ill.","kind":"worked"},{"id":"b32","section":"senses","cat":"samesame","text":"A cat sees far better than you in near-dark — but you see far more color.","animal":"tiger","kind":"found"},{"section":"senses","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"The genuinely sharp part of your sight is about the size of your thumbnail held at arm's length.","tryit":"Hold a book at arm's length, stare hard at one word and do not let your eyes drift. Now try to read the words on either side of it. You cannot. Everything outside that tiny patch is rough, and your eyes hide it by darting about constantly.","id":"b84"},{"section":"senses","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Stare at a color long enough and your eye will print the opposite one onto a blank wall.","tryit":"Stare at something strongly colored — a red book, a bright blue screen — for 30 seconds without moving your eyes. Then look at a white wall. A ghost of it floats there in the opposite color, because the cells handling that color are worn out for a moment.","id":"b85"},{"section":"senses","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"About 1 in 12 boys and 1 in 200 girls cannot tell certain reds and greens apart. The instructions for those color detectors sit on the X chromosome, and boys have only one X.","id":"b86"},{"section":"senses","cat":"created","kind":"found","text":"Inside your ear is a coiled tube laid out like a piano keyboard. High notes shake the hairs at one end, low notes at the far end, and every spot along it has its own line reporting to the brain.","more":"Cochlear implants use that map directly. An electrode is threaded along the coil, and it is told to stimulate the high-note end for high sounds and the far end for low ones.","id":"b87"},{"section":"senses","cat":"build","kind":"worked","text":"Your nose carries about 400 different types of smell detector. You can tell apart far more smells than that. So a smell cannot be one key fitting one lock. Each smell has to be a pattern across many detectors at once.","id":"b88"},{"section":"senses","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Your nose stops reporting any smell that does not change. Sniff a lemon or a marker pen steadily for a minute and it fades to almost nothing; take it away and it comes back at full strength. It is why you cannot smell your own house and other people can.","id":"b89"},{"section":"senses","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"You can hear higher notes than the adults around you. The top of the range starts slipping from the late teens, and by 50 most people have lost everything above about 12,000 vibrations a second.","more":"Some shops have played a 17,000-per-second tone outside to move teenagers along. The staff and the older customers will not hear it at all.","id":"b90"},{"section":"sleep","cat":"sleep","kind":"found","text":"Your body clock keeps running without daylight, but it drifts. Michel Siffre spent two months alone in a cave with no clock and no sunlight, and his sleep-and-wake rhythm settled into a day slightly longer than 24 hours.","more":"He also lost count. When his team told him the two months were up, Siffre thought about a month less had gone by. Left to itself a body keeps decent time, but a mind keeps a terrible calendar.","id":"b119"},{"section":"sleep","cat":"sleep","kind":"worked","text":"There is no single settled answer for why you sleep. The strongest evidence points at memory. The patterns your brain fired while learning get replayed overnight, and people tested after a night's sleep beat people tested after the same gap awake.","id":"b120"},{"section":"sleep","cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"Overnight, your brain gets rinsed out. The cells shrink back, the gaps between them widen, and fluid washes through carrying off the day's waste. The flow is far stronger asleep than it ever is awake.","more":"It was first traced in mice, with dye pumped through and filmed. Among the trash being carried off are the proteins that clog up the brains of people with dementia, which is one reason a missed night is not free.","id":"b121"},{"section":"sleep","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Roughly every 90 minutes your eyes start darting about under closed lids. Your brain is running almost as hard as it does awake. That is when most dreaming happens, about two hours a night. Your muscles are switched off, so you cannot act it out.","more":"Wake somebody during one of those bursts and about four times in five they report a dream. Wake them between bursts and they usually have nothing. Everyone dreams. Most of it is simply never filed.","id":"b122"},{"section":"sleep","cat":"senses","kind":"found","text":"Light sets your body clock, but not through the cells you see with. A separate set of cells sits at the back of your eye. All they do is report how bright it is. They report straight to a small clump of cells in the middle of your brain. That clump keeps the schedule.","id":"b123"},{"section":"sleep","cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A newborn sleeps 14 to 17 hours out of 24, in scraps of two or three hours at a time. A teenager needs 8 to 10 hours in one block. The amount you need drops steadily while you grow, then stops changing for the rest of your life.","id":"b124"},{"section":"sleep","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Melatonin is the hormone that tells your body it is night. Measured in a lab, its evening rise arrives one to two hours later in a teenager than in a younger child. A teenager still wide awake at eleven is following a clock, not dodging one.","id":"b125"},{"section":"sleep","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Falling asleep for as little as fifteen seconds can hand you an answer you could not find awake.","tryit":"Sit in a chair holding a spoon over a metal tray, and let yourself doze. The moment you genuinely drop off, your grip goes and the clatter wakes you. Edison and the painter Salvador Dali both napped this way on purpose.","more":"Researchers tested it properly. They gave volunteers a math puzzle with a hidden shortcut in it. Volunteers who had dipped into that first shallow stage of sleep spotted the shortcut 83% of the time. Volunteers who stayed awake managed 30%. Sinking into deeper sleep wiped the advantage out.","id":"b126"},{"section":"sleep","cat":"created","kind":"record","text":"Rest is written into the very first week of Genesis: six days of work, and a seventh set aside. Your body keeps a smaller version of the same pattern every single night, and it will not let you skip it for long.","id":"b127"},{"id":"b50","section":"growing","cat":"weird","text":"You grow fastest while you are asleep. Literally overnight.","more":"Most growth hormone is released during deep sleep. You are also measurably taller in the morning than at bedtime, because the discs in your spine decompress overnight.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b51","section":"growing","cat":"tryit","text":"You shrink a little every single day.","tryit":"Measure your height first thing in the morning, then again just before bed. Most people lose up to a centimeter over the day as the cushions between the bones of the spine squash down.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b52","section":"growing","cat":"babies","text":"Every person started as a single cell, too small to see without a microscope.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b53","section":"growing","cat":"weird","text":"Babies have no kneecaps at birth — not bony ones anyway.","more":"They start as cartilage, the same bendy stuff as the tip of your nose, and harden into bone over several years. Bendy knees survive a lot of crawling.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b54","section":"growing","cat":"sleep","text":"Nine to 12 hours is the figure for a nine-year-old — worked out by testing how children think and feel on more sleep and on less. Adults need less, which is deeply unfair.","more":"Your body clock also shifts later as you become a teenager. The urge to stay up is not laziness. The timing genuinely moves.","kind":"worked"},{"id":"b55","section":"growing","cat":"weird","text":"You had more taste buds as a toddler than you do now, spread over a wider area.","more":"Which is a real reason strong flavors felt overwhelming when you were small, and why sprouts get less offensive as you grow.","kind":"found"},{"section":"growing","cat":"created","kind":"found","text":"Your long bones do not grow all over. Each has a thin plate of soft cartilage near the end, and new bone is added there and nowhere else, so the shaft lengthens without being rebuilt. When you have finished growing, the plate seals over.","more":"A doctor can x-ray a child's hand and see how much growing is left from how open those plates still are. It is called bone age, and it can differ from the age on the birthday card.","id":"b112"},{"section":"growing","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"An x-ray of a six-year-old's jaw shows two complete sets of teeth. The baby ones are in place. The adult ones are stacked in the bone behind and below, waiting their turn.","more":"A baby tooth does not get pushed out with its roots intact. The roots dissolve away first, which is why a tooth that has been wobbly for a week comes out as a small hollow cap.","id":"b113"},{"section":"growing","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"In a growth spurt the ends go first. Hands and feet reach adult size before the arms and legs, which finish before the body does — which is why shoes stop fitting before trousers do.","id":"b114"},{"section":"growing","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"The tallest person ever measured was Robert Wadlow, at 8 ft 11.1 in (2.72 m) when he was last measured in June 1940. His growing never switched off — a gland under his brain kept releasing growth hormone.","id":"b115"},{"section":"growing","cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A newborn's head is about a quarter of its whole length. Yours is nearer an eighth. The head changes least because the brain does most of its growing earliest, and the rest of the body spends years catching up.","id":"b116"},{"section":"growing","cat":"babies","kind":"found","text":"A newborn can cry hard and produce no tears at all. The glands make just enough to keep the eyes wet; the flood that runs down cheeks usually does not arrive for several weeks.","id":"b117"},{"section":"growing","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"Your arm span comes out very close to your height.","tryit":"Stand with your back to a wall, arms straight out sideways, and get someone to mark where your fingertips reach. Measure between the marks, then measure your height. They land within an inch (2-3 cm) for most people, and children usually measure a shade narrower than they are tall.","id":"b118"},{"id":"b33","section":"skin","cat":"record","text":"Skin is your largest organ. Spread flat, an adult's is roughly the size of a bath towel.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b34","section":"skin","cat":"gross","text":"You shed skin cells constantly — a large share of household dust is you.","more":"You replace your outer layer of skin steadily, over roughly a month. The surface you are wearing now is not the one you had last term.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b35","section":"skin","cat":"tryit","text":"Your fingertips are far more sensitive than your back, and you can measure the difference.","tryit":"Bend a paperclip into a U with the ends about 1 cm apart. Touch both ends to a fingertip — you feel two points. Do the same on your forearm or back and it feels like one. Fingertips have far more touch sensors packed in.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b36","section":"skin","cat":"weird","text":"Fingers wrinkle in the bath because your nervous system does it, not because water soaks in. Nobody has caught the ridges doing a job; the tire-tread pattern is the argument that they grip.","more":"The nerves trigger tiny blood vessels under the skin to narrow, pulling the surface into ridges. Wrinkled fingertips grip wet things better — the pattern works like tire tread.","kind":"worked"},{"id":"b37","section":"skin","cat":"weird","text":"Goosebumps yank every hair on you upright. The same reflex puffs a cat's coat right up — which is the best read on what yours is doing, on hair far too fine to trap anything.","more":"Each tiny muscle pulls a hair upright. On a cat or a bird it traps warm air and makes the animal look bigger. On you it just makes bumps.","kind":"worked"},{"id":"b38","section":"skin","cat":"oops","text":"Itching has its own dedicated nerve pathway, separate from pain.","more":"Scratching works, but only briefly. It floods the spot with a different sensation. That drowns out the itch signal for a moment.","kind":"found"},{"section":"skin","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Shaving does not make hair grow back thicker, darker or faster. Mildred Trotter tested it in 1928, and every careful study since has found the same nothing.","more":"An uncut hair tapers to a fine soft point. Shaving slices it flat, so what you feel next is a blunt end — stubbly to the touch and darker-looking because it catches the light differently. Same hair.","id":"b98"},{"section":"skin","cat":"defence","kind":"found","text":"You do not lose most of your heat through your head. Your head is about a tenth of your skin, and it loses about a tenth of your heat.","more":"The claim traces to an old cold-weather test where volunteers were zipped into arctic suits with only their heads left bare. Of course the head lost the heat — it was the only part uncovered.","id":"b99"},{"section":"skin","cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"Sweat itself smells of almost nothing. The smell is made by bacteria living on your skin, feeding on what comes out of the glands and giving off the pongy chemicals themselves.","id":"b100"},{"section":"skin","cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"Your skin is waterproof both ways at once: bath water stays out, your own water stays in. The outer layer is dead flat cells stacked like bricks in greasy mortar. You can tell that layer is what matters — burns that destroy it leave a person drying out.","id":"b101"},{"section":"skin","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Your fingerprints belong to you alone. A koala's look so like a human's that forensic scientists have struggled to tell the two apart under a microscope. Outside the monkeys and apes, koalas are the only animals known to have them.","animal":"koala","id":"b102"},{"section":"skin","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"There is a patch of your skin that barely notices a hard pinch.","tryit":"Pinch the loose skin over the point of your elbow as hard as you like. Now pinch the same amount of skin on your forearm. The elbow skin is thicker, fattier and much shorter on pain endings, so it hardly registers at all.","id":"b103"},{"section":"skin","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Your fingernails grow about 1/8 inch (3.5 mm) a month, more than twice as fast as your toenails. The nail on your middle finger is usually the quickest and your little finger the slowest.","id":"b104"},{"id":"b39","section":"gut","cat":"gross","text":"Your stomach acid is strong enough to damage metal. Your stomach relines itself so that it does not digest you.","more":"A layer of mucus and a fresh supply of cells replace the lining continuously. Without that, the acid would start on the stomach wall itself.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b40","section":"gut","cat":"size","text":"Your small intestine is not small. Stretched out, it is several times your own height.","more":"It is called \"small\" because it is narrow, not short. Its inner surface is folded and fringed into millions of tiny projections, which is how so much absorbing surface fits inside you.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b41","section":"gut","cat":"weird","text":"Swallowing is not gravity. Muscles squeeze food along in a wave, so you could swallow upside down.","more":"That wave is called peristalsis, and it runs the whole length of your gut. Astronauts eat and drink normally in freefall for exactly this reason.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b42","section":"gut","cat":"gross","text":"Your gut is home to trillions of bacteria, and you need them.","more":"They help break down food your own enzymes cannot handle and make certain vitamins for you. Carried together they weigh a surprising amount.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b43","section":"gut","cat":"tryit","text":"Your mouth starts digesting before you swallow.","tryit":"Chew a plain cracker slowly and keep it in your mouth for a full minute without swallowing. It turns sweet. An enzyme in your saliva is already cutting the starch into sugar.","kind":"found"},{"id":"b44","section":"gut","cat":"samesame","text":"A cow needs four stomach chambers to get through grass. You get by with one.","animal":"reindeer","kind":"found"},{"section":"gut","cat":"record","kind":"record","text":"Alexis St Martin was shot in the side in 1822. He survived, but the wound left a hole into his stomach that never fully closed. His doctor was William Beaumont. For years he lowered food into the hole on a string, then pulled it back out to see how far it had got.","more":"Over 250 experiments. It is how anyone first learned that a stomach works by chemistry rather than by grinding. It was hard on St Martin, who put up with being an experiment for much of his life.","id":"b91"},{"section":"gut","cat":"build","kind":"found","text":"Your gut runs on its own nerve network of roughly 500 million cells, more than your spinal cord holds. Cut its line to the brain and the gut carries on digesting anyway.","id":"b92"},{"section":"gut","cat":"tryit","kind":"found","text":"You can time your own digestion using sweetcorn.","tryit":"Eat a good helping of sweetcorn and write down the time. The yellow skins are made of a fiber you cannot break down, so they come out looking exactly the same. Watch for them. For most people they show up somewhere around a day to a day and a half later.","id":"b93"},{"section":"gut","cat":"food","kind":"found","text":"You make somewhere between 1 and 3 pints (0.5 to 1.5 L) of spit a day and swallow nearly all of it. It is not just water — it carries enzymes, germ-killers, and minerals that patch tiny damage on your teeth.","id":"b94"},{"section":"gut","cat":"gross","kind":"found","text":"A rumbling stomach is not asking to be fed. About every 90 minutes, when there is nothing to digest, a slow wave sweeps down your gut clearing out leftovers. The noise is gas and liquid being shoved along ahead of it.","id":"b95"},{"section":"gut","cat":"created","kind":"worked","text":"The appendix gets called a spare part. It is a narrow dead-end pocket off the main tube. The reasoning goes like this. When illness flushes the gut out, useful bacteria tucked up that pocket are not swept away, and they can restock everything afterwards.","more":"Nobody has watched it happen inside a person. The argument is the shape — a pouch too narrow to flush out, lined with immune tissue that appears to shelter those bacteria rather than attack them.","id":"b96"},{"section":"gut","cat":"weird","kind":"found","text":"Your liver does hundreds of separate jobs and is the one large organ that grows back. Remove two thirds of it and the remainder returns to full size. It is why a living person can donate part of a liver: both halves regrow.","id":"b97"}];
globalThis.BODY = BODY;
